mes>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>In-vitro analysis for microbial barrier properties of 2-octyl cyanoacrylate-derived wound treatment films</article-title><source>J Cutan Med Surg</source><year>2003</year><volume>7</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>13</fpage><lpage>9</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12447623</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Cass</surname><given-names>DL</given-names></name><name><surname>Meuli</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Adzick</surname><given-names>NS</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Scar wars: implications of fetal wound healing for the pediatric burn patient</article-title><source>Pediatr Surg Int</source><year>1997</year><volume>12</volume><issue>7</issue><fpage>484</fpage><lpage>9</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9238112</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lorenz</surname><given-names>HP</given-names></name><name><surname>Adzick</surname><given-names>NS</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Scarless skin wound repair in the fetus</article-title><source>West J Med.</source><fpage>350</fpage><lpage>5</lpage><comment>1993;159(special issue):</comment></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Adzick</surname><given-names>NS</given-names></name><name><surname>Lorenz</surname><given-names>HP</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Cells, matrix, growth factors, and the surgeon. The biology of scarless fetal wound repair</article-title><source>Ann Surg</source><year>1994</year><volume>220</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>10</fpage><lpage>8</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8024353</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>The female newborn, weighing 3.25 kg, accidentally sustained a laceration over the right side of the face and temporal region.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="eplasty09e03_fig1"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><graphic xlink:href="eplasty09e03_fig2"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>